WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.858
This is money. The round ones are
coins. The flat ones are notes.

2
00:00:09.078 --> 00:00:14.326
Every piece has its own value. Count them
all up, and you know what the purse is worth.

3
00:00:14.826 --> 00:00:19.934
Our money is shillings. Every coin
and note has its value printed on it.

4
00:00:20.154 --> 00:00:24.427
These are the coins: one, five,
ten, twenty and fifty shillings.

5
00:00:24.647 --> 00:00:30.591
This flat one is a note. A note is worth
more than most coins. This one is 100.

6
00:00:30.811 --> 00:00:36.291
A big coin is not always worth
more. Read the number, not the size.

7
00:00:36.791 --> 00:00:40.506
Here is a purse with money
in it. What is it all worth?

8
00:00:40.726 --> 00:00:45.417
Always start with the biggest
piece. Here the biggest is the 50.

9
00:00:45.637 --> 00:00:49.027
Then count on. Fifty, and
twenty more makes seventy.

10
00:00:49.247 --> 00:00:53.798
Five more makes seventy-five.
So the purse holds 75 sh.

11
00:00:54.018 --> 00:00:58.615
Three pieces, and 75 shillings.
Count the value, not the pieces.

12
00:00:59.115 --> 00:01:02.970
Now you choose the money
yourself. Make 35 sh exactly.

13
00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:06.580
Take a 20, then a 10,
then a 5. That is 35.

14
00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:10.004
Say the total as you go:
twenty, thirty, thirty-five.

15
00:01:10.224 --> 00:01:13.615
Now make 70 sh, with as
few pieces as you can.

16
00:01:13.835 --> 00:01:18.943
A 50 fits, and leaves 20. A
20 finishes it, so two pieces.

17
00:01:19.163 --> 00:01:23.621
Seven 10 shilling coins make 70
too, but that is seven pieces.

18
00:01:24.121 --> 00:01:26.954
Money has its own way
of being written down.

19
00:01:27.174 --> 00:01:31.446
Forty-five shillings is written
45 sh, with the sh after it.

20
00:01:31.666 --> 00:01:36.171
A shop may write KSh 45
instead, with the KSh in front.

21
00:01:36.391 --> 00:01:40.571
Both mean the same money. Read the
number first, then the letters.

22
00:01:41.071 --> 00:01:43.857
Here are two purses.
Which one is worth more?

23
00:01:44.077 --> 00:01:49.093
Purse A holds a 20, a 20
and a 5. Add them up: 45 sh.

24
00:01:49.313 --> 00:01:53.910
Purse B holds just one
coin, a 50. That is 50 sh.

25
00:01:54.130 --> 00:01:58.867
Purse B has fewer pieces and more
money. More coins is not more money.

26
00:01:59.367 --> 00:02:03.686
You have 35 sh, and the
thing you want costs 50 sh.

27
00:02:03.906 --> 00:02:06.878
Count on from what you
have, up to the price.

28
00:02:07.098 --> 00:02:10.860
Hop 5 to reach 40. Then
hop 10 to reach 50.

29
00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:15.399
The hops are 5 and 10. So
you need 15 shillings more.

30
00:02:15.899 --> 00:02:19.382
Count money biggest first,
then count on from there.

31
00:02:19.602 --> 00:02:24.710
One amount can be made in many ways. The
fewest pieces uses the biggest coins.

32
00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:29.295
45 sh and KSh 45 are two
ways to write the same money.

33
00:02:29.515 --> 00:02:33.649
Compare purses by value, and
count on to find how much more.
